t deployment — E006; SSAR de-specified — no Kubernetes API mechanics dictated, workspace authorization reuses existing auth plugin infrastructure). The strategy body already incorporates these; no divergence between SME input and the technical approach as written.\n\nNow write everything. Files:\n- artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md (body then frontmatter via script)\n- E001..E006 .md files + frontmatter\n- 6 ai-signals files\n\nLet me now write the files. Bodies first (no frontmatter), then set frontmatter via script.\n\nFor E004's gated_by — single value E002. But wait: E004's direct deps are E002 (Investigation) and E005 (Implementation). gated_by=E002 ✓.\n\nOne more check — the prompt says for gated epics: \"Every downstream epic gated by an Investigation is a true gate (remove if the epic may not exist, rewrite if scope/approach changes, add_remediation...)\". E004: rewrite. E005: rewrite. E006: rewrite.\n\nE005 gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach: \"If the workspace store initialization is already decoupled from tracking store availability, the epic reduces to verification and regression coverage; if the coupling is deeper than an independent initialization path can address, re-plan the workspace resolution approach for the artifacts-only context before implementing.\"\n\nNow let me also decide E004 title: \"Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\". E005: \"Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\". E006: \"Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator\".\n\nInvestigation titles:\n- E001: \"Investigate workspace resolution initialization dependencies in the artifacts-only server context\"\n- E002: \"Investigate artifact request path resolvability to workspace membership\"\n- E003: \"Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\"\n\nComponent field: \"MLflow\" for all six. Summary notes mapping.\n\nTeams: \"MLflow Team\" for all.\n\nNow write. Also note: the artifact body must NOT reference sibling epic IDs (E001 etc.) — including in the summary? No, the summary is fine (it's the plan with the Epic List table). Only epic bodies must avoid them.\n\nAlso each epic body Title as one line (e.g., \"# Workspace-aware ...\").\n\nLet me write the decomposition summary first.\n\nFor HLR IDs: strategy lists HLRs as bullets without IDs. I'll label them HLR-1..HLR-8 in order.\n\nBody of summary:\n\n# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009\n\n## Epic List\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\nE001 ... etc.\n\n## Dependency DAG\nmermaid\n\n## DAG Justification\ntable: Edge | Rule | Rationale\nRows: E001→E005, E002→E004, E005→E004, E003→E006, plus parallel notes (E001∥E002∥E003; E004∥E006).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\nHLR-1 P0 E004\nHLR-2 P0 E004, E005\nHLR-3 P0 E004\nHLR-4 P0 E004\nHLR-5 P1 E006\nHLR-6 P1 E006\nHLR-7 P1 E005 (raised to P0 by inheritance — enables HLR-2)\nHLR-8 P2 E004 — bundled: ...\n\n## Investigation Derivation\nBrief: which open questions/assumptions became which investigations and which were not.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\nLog.\n\n## Component Mapping\nNote canonical component.\n\n## Health Warnings\nList.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\nNot applicable — all dependencies internal and existing; no uncertain-timing external dependency. State that.\n\nNow write all files. Let me be efficient — use Write tool for each body, then frontmatter.py set commands in one bash call.\n\nSignal rationale files format: \"markdown table with Signal, Value, and Rationale columns\".\n\nLet me write the epic bodies carefully avoiding sibling IDs.\n\nEpic E001 body:\n\n# Investigate workspace resolution initialization dependencies in the artifacts-only server context\n\n## Description\nResolves whether the workspace resolution provided by the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin — the `kubernetes-auth` workspace provider registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry — can be initialized and exercised in an artifacts-only MLflow server where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. This is the strategy's flagged risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\" and open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" The outcome determines the scope of plugin refactoring needed and the feasibility of initializing only the workspace provider in the artifacts-only server.\n\n## Scope\n- Map the initialization path of the kubernetes workspace store (`kubernetes://` URI scheme) and the kubernetes-auth plugin's workspace resolution: what it imports, what it requires, and whether any step depends on tracking store or model registry store availability.\n- Determine whether experiment-to-workspace resolution can be exercised in a server context with no tracking/registry stores initialized.\n- If coupled: identify the concrete refactoring needed to factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, and assess its size.\n- Deliver findings as a documented determination with code-level evidence and a refactoring scope assessment usable for planning the plugin and server implementation work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written determination, with code-level references, of whether workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depends on tracking store availability, answering the strategy's open question with a definitive yes/no.\n- If coupled: a refactoring plan identifying what must move to an independent initialization path, with the affected surfaces enumerated.\n- If not coupled: verification evidence (e.g. a runnable reproduction) that workspace resolution works without tracking/registry store initialization.\n- A recommendation on whether the plugin requires extension or only verification for the artifacts-only context, stated in pass/fail terms usable for scheduling the downstream work.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Enables HLR-2 (P0, workspace-membership validation of artifact requests) and HLR-7 (P1, plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context) by resolving the unknown that determines their implementation scope.\n- Derives from the strategy's risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\" and dependency note on mlflow-kubernetes-plugins.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Investigate artifact request path resolvability to workspace membership\n\n## Description\nResolves whether artifact request paths handled by the artifacts-only serving endpoint carry sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership without initializing tracking stores. Artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths (for the standard S3 artifact store, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), and the workspace-aware artifacts-only server must resolve each artifact path to its parent experiment and then to the experiment's workspace assignment. The outcome determines the workspace-validation approach in the MLflow server implementation; the strategy flags this both as an assumption needing validation and as the risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\".\n\n## Scope\n- Trace how artifact request paths are constructed and interpreted by the artifacts-only proxy for the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration), and confirm whether the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure reliably yields the parent experiment ID.\n- Determine whether run-ID-only paths can be resolved to their parent experiment without a tracking store, and what is required if not.\n- Identify artifact root configurations where path-to-workspace resolution fails (custom artifact roots, non-S3 backends) and document them as limitations, per the strategy's mitigation of scoping the initial implementation to the S3 artifact store.\n- Deliver a validated path-resolution approach with pass/fail criteria for the server-side workspace validation implementation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written, code-referenced determination of whether artifact request paths include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership, answering the strategy's assumption with a definitive result.\n- A validated approach for resolving an artifact request path to workspace membership in a server context without tracking stores, demonstrated against the S3 artifact store layout.\n- A documented list of artifact-store configurations where resolution is not reliable, with the limitation stated for documentation in the implementation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Enables HLR-1 and HLR-2 (P0, workspace-validated artifact serving) by determining the path-resolution approach the server implementation builds on.\n- Derives from the strategy's assumption \"Artifact request paths ... include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership\" and the risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\".\n\nE003 body:\n\n# Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\nResolves how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. This is the strategy's open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated artifacts-only instance?\" — the answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. The outcome determines the routing approach implemented in the operator's split-server topology work.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluate routing options for directing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment's ingress to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, within the operator's existing Gateway API HTTPRoute pattern (HTTPRoutes are created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery).\n- Determine whether the main tracking server needs a configuration change (or code change) to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance, and identify the specific configuration surface if so.\n- Produce a design decision with rationale that the operator implementation can build against, covering HTTPRoute structure and any tracking-deployment configuration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A documented routing design describing how artifact traffic is routed from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, consistent with the operator's conditional Gateway API HTTPRoute pattern.\n- A definitive determination of whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, with the configuration surface identified if yes.\n- The design decision recorded with rationale and trade-offs, in a form the split-topology operator implementation can directly apply.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- Enables HLR-5 and HLR-6 (P1, dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment and artifact traffic routing) by selecting the routing approach.\n- Derives from the strategy's open question on artifact traffic routing configuration.\n\nE005 body:\n\n# Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n\n## Description\nMakes the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin's workspace resolution function correctly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The plugin already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking server's non-artifact operations, registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry; this epic verifies that resolution in the artifacts-only context and extends it where the resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization path. Workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure — no new Kubernetes API authorization mechanics are dictated at strategy level. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution in a server context with no tracking/registry stores initialized, per the findings on initialization dependencies.\n- Where resolution is coupled to the full server initialization path, factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n- Preserve the existing integration surface: registration through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loading via WorkspaceStoreRegistry, so the tracking server and the artifacts-only server load the same plugin.\n- Maintain existing tracking-server workspace resolution behavior (no regression for non-artifact operations).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The plugin's workspace resolution correctly resolves experiment-to-workspace mapping in a server context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized (HLR-7).\n- Workspace resolution used by the tracking server's non-artifact operations is unchanged, verified by the plugin's existing test suite.\n- The plugin loads through the existing entry point and registry mechanism with no new registration surface.\n- If refactoring was required: the independent initialization path is covered by tests demonstrating resolution without tracking store availability.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-7 (P1): auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization. (Raised to P0 by priority inheritance: this capability enables the P0 workspace-membership validation of artifact requests, HLR-2.)\n- Enables HLR-2 (P0): the server's per-request workspace validation delegates to this resolution capability.\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\nMakes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation receive HTTP 403. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags, per Staff Engineer / SME input. This epic builds on the plugin's workspace resolution capability for the artifacts-only context (delivered elsewhere in this strategy) and on the validated artifact path resolution approach.\n\n## Scope\n- Allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error when missing) and initialize only the workspace provider.\n- Keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized in this mode; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment, look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny with HTTP 403 on failed workspace validation.\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unaffected.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (HLR-8, P2 — bundled here because it hooks the same artifact-request denial path the P0 validation work produces; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface).\n- Measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation to inform the product decision on acceptable overhead (the strategy sets no latency target; performance optimization is out of scope).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Starting the server with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` and no `--workspace-store-uri` fails with a clear error at startup.\n- With `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, the server initializes only the workspace provider; tracking and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved); the single-tenant/backwards-compatibility regression suite passes.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit log entries in the server log.\n- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported in the epic's test report (no target is set by the strategy; the measurement informs the pending product decision).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled, not separately schedulable: it instruments the same artifact-request denial path built by the P0 validation work; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface.\n\nE006 body:\n\n# Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator\n\n## Description\nExtends the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and to configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that dedicated instance — a split-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration toggle (per Staff Engineer / SME input). The main tracking server continues to handle experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry; the artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. The operator uses its existing Helm-in-operator pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply) and propagates workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance using the same mechanisms as for the main tracking deployment. Gateway API HTTPRoute resources are created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n\n## Scope\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, separate from the main tracking Deployment's resources.\n- Configuration of the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the routing design selected under this strategy.\n- Propagation of workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the operator's existing configuration mechanisms; the artifacts-only Deployment runs with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`.\n- Operator validation that rejects configurations where the artifacts-only instance would be deployed without the workspace flags (so artifact requests cannot bypass workspace validation through misconfiguration).\n- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar deployed on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment (authentication layer unchanged).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciliation creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment, verified by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command includes `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, verified by inspecting the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The main tracking deployment is configured to route artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing design, and artifact requests through the main tracking deployment's route reach the dedicated instance.\n- The operator rejects (with a clear status condition) MLflow CR configurations that enable the artifacts-only instance without the workspace flags.\n- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created only when the Gateway API CRD is present at runtime, consistent with the operator's conditional CRD discovery pattern.\n- The rendered topology is idempotent under repeated reconciliation (server-side apply produces no spurious changes).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute.\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\nHmm — E006 is P1 and doesn't block P0 epics. ✓.\n\nWait, one thing about E004's dependency on E005 — E004 is P0, E005 is P0 ✓. E005 deps E001 (P0 Investigation) ✓. E004 deps E002 (P0) ✓. E006 (P1) deps E003 (P1) ✓.\n\nNow signal files.\n\nE001-ai-signals.md (investigation_signals):\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n| Question specificity | 1 | Concrete, enumerable questions from the strategy's open question and risk: does workspace store initialization depend on tracking store availability; can resolution run without stores. Pass/fail is a definitive yes/no with a refactoring-scope assessment. |\n| Source accessibility | 1 | The answer is determinable from readable source: the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package (published v1.5.0, referenced in mlflow.md) and the MLflow server initialization code in the accessible midstream/downstream repos. |\n| Local runnability | 0 | The kubernetes:// workspace store resolves against the Kubernetes API; there is no embedded/bundled server equivalent, so exercising resolution end-to-end needs a cluster or API-server stand-in. Static init-dependency analysis is local, but runnability of the component itself is cluster-bound. |\n| Cluster/hardware dependence | -1 | The gating question (init coupling) is answerable from source; peripheral confirmation that workspace resolution works against a live workspace store needs a cluster with the Kubernetes API. |\n| Human judgment required | 0 | Purely technical question owned by the MLflow Team; no stakeholder or external-party decision gates the answer. |\n\nE002: qs=1 (concrete: do paths carry experiment/run IDs; S3 layout; definitive result), sa=1 (mlflow source: artifacts endpoint + artifact path construction readable), lr=1 (artifact path handling exercisable locally as a pip library against local-filesystem/S3-compatible stores; no cluster needed), chd=0 (path-structure analysis requires no live cluster), hjr=0.\n\nE003: qs=0 (design question — options enumerable but criterion is a design choice, not a pass/fail test), sa=1 (operator repo's routing/controller code and Gateway API HTTPRoute patterns are readable and determine the design), lr=0 (HTTPRoute routing behavior needs a cluster with Gateway API; the components cannot be exercised as plain libraries), chd=-1 (validating the chosen routing against a live Gateway API needs a cluster; the gating design question itself is source-based), hjr=0 (technical design decision within the MLflow Team; no stakeholder or external-party input required).\n\nE004-ai-signals.md (ai_signals):\n| Change specificity | 1 | Exact flags (--artifacts-only, --enable-workspaces, --workspace-store-uri), endpoint behavior (HTTP 403/503), and per-request validation flow are specified by the strategy. |\n| Pattern precedent | 1 | Workspace validation already exists in the same server for non-artifact operations via --enable-workspaces and the kubernetes-auth plugin (mlflow.md). |\n| Adapter pattern | 0 | No adapter/plugin reference implementation is being followed; the work extends the server's own request-handling path. |\n| Existing foundation | 1 | Extends the existing artifacts-only mode and existing workspace isolation; no greenfield surface. |\n| Open questions | -1 | Approach depends on unresolved investigations: artifact path resolvability and plugin initialization coupling both change the implementation approach. |\n| External dependency | 1 | No upstream contribution or vendor coordination; all dependencies are internal and exist. |\n| Human process gates | 0 | No epic-level human approval step; the strategy's pending reviews (architecture review etc.) are strategy-level gates, not steps inside this epic. |\n| Repo access | 1 | Target repo (red-hat-data-services/mlflow midstream/downstream) is clonable and modifiable. |\n| Architecture claims | 1 | mlflow.md corroborates the premise: the RHOAI MLflow server runs with --app-name kubernetes-auth, --enable-workspaces, --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// (Dockerfile.konflux CMD), with the kubernetes-auth plugin from mlflow-kubernetes-plugins v1.5.0 providing workspace-scoped authorization. |\n\nE005-ai-signals.md:\n| Change specificity | 0 | The what (workspace resolution in artifacts-only context) is stated, but file-level scope depends on the initialization-coupling investigation outcome — verify-only vs. refactor is not yet determined. |\n| Pattern precedent | 1 | The plugin already implements workspace resolution for the tracking server's non-artifact operations; the epic extends/refactors that existing code. |\n| Adapter pattern | 1 | Follows the plugin's existing reference implementation: registration via the mlflow.workspace_provider entry point group and loading via WorkspaceStoreRegistry (mlflow.md). |\n| Existing foundation | 1 | Extends an existing, deployed plugin (v1.5.0). |\n| Open questions | -1 | Scope is gated by the initialization-coupling investigation; the refactoring approach changes with its outcome. |\n| External dependency | 1 | Internal component (strategy dependency table: internal, exists). |\n| Human process gates | 0 | No human approval step inside the epic. |\n| Repo access | 0 | The plugin is consumed as a published PyPI package (mlflow.md); its source repository is not listed in architecture context, so AI repo access is unconfirmed rather than known-good. |\n| Architecture claims | 1 | mlflow.md corroborates the plugin's existence, version (v1.5.0), registration mechanism, and role in workspace-scoped authorization, grounding this epic's premise. |\n\nE006-ai-signals.md:\n| Change specificity | 1 | Specific deliverables: new Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute chart templates, exact flag set for the artifacts-only container command, routing configuration, and operator validation rules. |\n| Pattern precedent | 1 | The operator already renders Deployments, Services, and conditional HTTPRoutes through its Helm-in-operator pattern (mlflow-operator.md). |\n| Adapter pattern | 0 | No adapter/plugin pattern; chart templates follow the operator's existing template conventions (covered by precedent). |\n| Existing foundation | 1 | Extends the existing operator and chart; no new component onboarding. |\n| Open questions | -1 | The artifact traffic routing design is unresolved and changes the HTTPRoute/routing approach. |\n| External dependency | 1 | Gateway API is an existing internal platform dependency with runtime CRD discovery; no upstream contribution needed. |\n| Human process gates | 0 | No epic-level human approval step. |\n| Repo access | 1 | Target repo (red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator) is clonable and modifiable. |\n| Architecture claims | 1 | mlflow-operator.md corroborates the Helm-in-operator pattern (CR spec → Helm values → templates → Server-Side Apply), conditional Gateway API HTTPRoute creation via runtime CRD discovery, and the MLflow CR surface this epic extends. |\n\nNow the decomposition summary. Let me write it.\n\nCritical path: E001 → E005 → E004 (3 epics). critical_path_length=3. epic_count=6.\n\nAlso include in summary: note about OQ1 and OQ4 handling, and OQ2/OQ3 mapped to investigations. OQ2 = routing (E003), OQ3 = plugin init (E001). OQ1 latency → health warning + measurement AC. OQ4 audit channel → health warning + bundled HLR-8.\n\nAlso assumptions handling: five \"needs validation\" assumptions — #1 (flags composable) → validated within E004 implementation (its own work); #2 (workspace store mapping without tracking stores) → E001; #3