server implementation through the plugin capability it builds on; no direct edge needed. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | Path resolvability determines the workspace-validation approach in the server. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E005 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The server's per-request workspace validation delegates to the plugin's workspace resolution capability; the server builds against that capability (library → consumer, serial). |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The routing design determines the HTTPRoute structure and whether tracking-deployment configuration changes are needed. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | — | Three independent unknowns resolved by different experiments; parallel starts. |\n| E004 ∥ E006 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared build-against artifact) | The operator consumes the server's flag combination as configuration references rendered into Deployment specs — an AC gate (rendered container command, end-to-end routing check), not a DAG edge. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n| HLR-1 flags combinable | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-2 workspace-membership validation, 403 | P0 | E004, E005 | E004 enforces per-request validation; E005 delivers the plugin resolution it delegates to. |\n| HLR-3 fail-closed on unreachable workspace store | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-4 single-tenant / no-workspace-flag behavior unchanged | P0 | E004 | Backwards-compatibility regression ACs. |\n| HLR-5 dedicated artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 | |\n| HLR-6 main tracking routes artifact traffic | P1 | E006 | Routing design from E003. |\n| HLR-7 plugin resolution in artifacts-only context | P1 | E005 | Raised to P0 by priority inheritance: enables the P0 workspace-membership validation (HLR-2). |\n| HLR-8 audit logging of denials via server logs | P2 | E004 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: it instruments the same artifact-request denial path the P0 validation work builds; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface (shared code surface, same endpoints). |\n\nAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Investigation Derivation\n| Strategy unknown | Handling |\n| Open question: workspace store init vs tracking store availability (+ risk \"Auth plugin coupling\", assumption \"workspace store mapping without tracking stores\") | E001 Investigation — outcome changes plugin/server approach. |\n| Assumption: artifact request paths carry experiment/run IDs (+ risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\") | E002 Investigation — outcome changes the validation approach. |\n| Open question: operator artifact traffic routing configuration | E003 Investigation — outcome changes HTTPRoute design and possibly tracking-deployment configuration. |\n| Open question: acceptable latency overhead (PM/Engineering) | Not an Investigation — performance optimization is out of scope (separate RFE per strategy); no latency target exists, so the answer cannot change which epics exist. E004 carries a latency-measurement AC to inform the decision; see Health Warnings. |\n| Open question: audit telemetry channel (PM/Security) | Not an Investigation — HLR-8 as written specifies server logs; if PM/Security selects a different channel the scope expands (see Health Warnings re-plan trigger). |\n| Assumption: Helm chart supports a second Deployment | Implementation detail within E006 (resolved by doing the work); noted as scope/AC. |\n| Assumption: single-tenant deployments don't set workspace flags | Backwards-compatibility ACs on E004. |\n| Assumption: flags composable without breaking individual operation | Validated within E004 (its core deliverable; failure = fix-and-retry within the same epic). |\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\nThe strategy's technical approach already incorporates all three SME directives; the decomposition follows them, and no divergence between SME input and the AI-generated approach remained to override:\n1. **No new flag** — E004 composes `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces`; no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field appears anywhere in the decomposition.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — E006 deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance (separate Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) and routes artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment.\n3. **SSAR detail over-specified** — no epic dictates SelfSubjectAccessReview or other Kubernetes API authorization mechanics; workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup is unchanged.\n\n## Component Mapping\nCanonical RHAI component list contains `MLflow` but not `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All six epics are filed under the canonical component `MLflow` (the single most-relevant canonical name); E005's work spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins and E006's spans the MLflow operator. Submit-time component routing relies on the team field (`MLflow Team` owns all three strategy components).\n\n## Health Warnings\n- **Epic size overage (E006)**: the operator split-topology epic is estimated at 2-3 sprints per the strategy's effort estimate, above the ~2-week epic guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is one logical change to the operator's Helm chart (templates + routing + flag propagation + validation would double-touch the same chart if split). Re-split trigger: re-split along the HLR-5/HLR-6 boundary (dedicated-instance deployment vs. artifact traffic routing) if the chart work for the second Deployment proves materially larger than estimated or if the routing design investigation concludes the tracking server itself needs code changes.\n- **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: the P0 server-side capability (E004) delivers deployed user value only through the P1 operator topology (E006) — the strategy labels the operator HLRs P1 while the RFE's multi-tenant artifact serving value materializes only when both land. Labels are respected as written; planning should note the coupling.\n- **Pending strategy-level process gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\" at strategy level; they gate the strategy, not individual epics.\n- **Latency target undefined (open question, PM/Engineering)**: no acceptable per-request validation overhead is set; performance optimization is out of scope (separate RFE). E004 measures and reports the overhead; if a target is later set that requires caching at launch, plan a follow-up epic (short-TTL caching is the strategy's noted mitigation).\n- **Audit channel open question (PM/Security)**: HLR-8 specifies server logs. If PM/Security selects OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log instead, E004's audit-logging scope expands — re-plan that portion when the decision lands.\n- **Scope trap noted (S3-only path resolution)**: per the strategy's risk mitigation, initial path-to-workspace resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store layout; non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation, not supported.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no uncertain-timing external dependency, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The investigations run in parallel at the start; server-side work (E005 → E004) and operator work (E006) proceed as independent streams.\n\nCareful with OVER_PAT: \"above the ~2-week epic guideline\" appears in Health Warnings — TRIG_PAT must match somewhere in summary: \"Re-split trigger: re-split along...\" contains \"re-split\" ✓.\n\nCheck NEG_PAT on the overage sentence: \"the operator split-topology epic is estimated at 2-3 sprints per the strategy's effort estimate, above the ~2-week epic guideline.\" — OVER_PAT: \"above ... guideline\" within 120 chars ✓. NEG_PAT words: \"no epic|none|within|under|...\" — hmm \"within\" not in that sentence. The sentence is split by (?<=[.!?])\\s+|\\n — the sentence is \"- **Epic size overage (E006)**: the operator split-topology epic is estimated at 2-3 sprints per the strategy's effort estimate, above the ~2-week epic guideline.\" No negation words. Overage flagged, TRIG present → OK.\n\nAlso \"Priority inversion risk\" sentence — check OVER_PAT doesn't accidentally fire elsewhere without... TRIG_PAT searches whole summary anyway, so any overage sentence is fine since \"re-split\" exists. OK.\n\nNow frontmatter for summary: parent_strat, epic_count=6, critical_path_length=3.\n\nEpic frontmatter:\nE001: epic_id, title, parent_strat, component=MLflow, team=MLflow Team, type=Investigation, priority=P0, dependencies=\"\" (empty list — how to pass? `dependencies=` maybe. Let me pass nothing? Default is []. I'll just omit dependencies for epics with none — default []. Or set dependencies=\"\" — might create empty string. Omit.), investigation_signals.*=...\n\nE002: same, P0.\nE003: same, P1.\nE004: type=Implementation, priority=P0, dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E002,RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\", gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\", gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite, gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"...\", ai_signals.*.\nE005: Implementation, P0, dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\", gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\", gate_failure_impact...\nE006: Implementation, P1, dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\", gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\", gate_failure_impact...\n\nHow does frontmatter.py set parse list fields? \"dependencies=\"{ID}-E002,{ID}-E003\"\" per the prompt example — comma-separated string converted to list. Good.\n\nLet me now write all the files. I'll write bodies with Write tool, then run frontmatter.py set for each, then batch-read, then validator, then decompose_complete=true.\n\nTitles (frontmatter title):\nE001: \"Investigate workspace re