{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-32db0668-7979-4436-978b-fd4ea4d8f82c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-953ac745c326463c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-ks66mr2k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace resolution initialization dependencies in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact request path resolvability to workspace membership | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigate plugin init coupling\"] --> E005[\"E005 Plugin workspace resolution (artifacts-only)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Investigate path resolvability\"] --> E004[\"E004 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\"]\n    E005 --> E004\n    E003[\"E003 Investigate routing design\"] --> E006[\"E006 Operator split-server topology\"]\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The initialization-coupling investigation determines whether the plugin needs refactoring or only verification — the scope and approach of the plugin implementation change with the outcome. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (transitive via E005) | The same outcome reaches the server implementation through the plugin capability it builds on; no direct edge is needed. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | Path resolvability determines the workspace-validation approach in the server (path → experiment → workspace). True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E005 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The server's per-request workspace validation delegates to the plugin's workspace resolution capability; the server builds against that capability (library → consumer, serial). |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The routing design determines the HTTPRoute structure and whether the tracking deployment needs configuration changes. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | — | Three independent unknowns resolved by different experiments with different deliverables; all start in parallel. |\n| E004 ∥ E006 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared build-against artifact) | The operator consumes the server's flag combination as configuration references rendered into Deployment specs — coupling is expressed as AC gates on E006 (rendered container command, end-to-end routing check), never as a DAG edge. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004, E005 | E004 enforces per-request validation; E005 delivers the plugin resolution it delegates to. |\n| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 | Backwards-compatibility regression ACs. |\n| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 | |\n| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance | P1 | E006 | Routing approach selected by E003. |\n| HLR-7: plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | E005 | Raised to P0 by priority inheritance: enables the P0 workspace-membership validation (HLR-2). |\n| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E004 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: it instruments the same artifact-request denial path the P0 validation work builds; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface and endpoints. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Investigation Derivation\n\n| Strategy unknown | Handling |\n|------------------|----------|\n| Open question: does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability (+ risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\", assumption \"workspace store mapping accessible without tracking stores\") | E001 Investigation — the outcome changes the plugin and server implementation approach. |\n| Assumption: artifact request paths carry sufficient information (experiment/run ID) to resolve workspace membership (+ risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\") | E002 Investigation — the outcome changes the workspace-validation approach. |\n| Open question: how should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment | E003 Investigation — the outcome changes the HTTPRoute design and possibly the tracking deployment's configuration. |\n| Open question: acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation (PM/Engineering) | Not an Investigation — performance optimization is out of scope (separate RFE per the strategy) and no latency target exists, so the answer cannot change which epics exist or what they build. E004 carries a latency-measurement AC to inform the decision; see Health Warnings. |\n| Open question: audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials (PM/Security) | Not an Investigation — HLR-8 as written specifies server logs, so the implementation is fully determined; if PM/Security later selects a different channel, the audit-logging portion of E004 expands (re-plan trigger in Health Warnings). |\n| Assumption: operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Implementation detail within E006, resolved by doing the work (failure = fix-and-retry within the same epic). |\n| Assumption: single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Backwards-compatibility ACs on E004. |\n| Assumption: flags composable without breaking either flag used individually | Validated within E004 — it is that epic's core deliverable. |\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nThe strategy's technical approach already incorporates all three SME directives; the decomposition follows them, and no divergence between the SME input and the AI-generated approach remained to override:\n\n1. **No new flag** — E004 composes `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag; no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field appears anywhere in this decomposition.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — E006 deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and routes artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment.\n3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — no epic dictates SelfSubjectAccessReview or other Kubernetes API authorization mechanics; workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup is unchanged.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nThe canonical RHAI component list contains `MLflow` but neither `MLflow Operator` nor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All six epics are therefore filed under the canonical component `MLflow` (the single most-relevant canonical name): E005's work spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins, and E006's spans the MLflow operator. The team field (`MLflow Team`, which owns all three strategy components) carries the routing information.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Epic size overage (E006)**: the operator split-topology epic is estimated at 2-3 sprints in the strategy's effort estimate, above the ~2-week epic guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is one logical change to the operator's Helm chart — templates, routing, flag propagation, and validation would double-touch the same chart if split. Re-split trigger: re-split along the HLR-5/HLR-6 boundary (dedicated-instance deployment vs. artifact traffic routing) if the chart work for the second Deployment proves materially larger than estimated, or if the routing design investigation concludes the tracking server itself needs code changes.\n- **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: the P0 server-side capability (E004) delivers deployed user value only through the P1 operator topology (E006) — the strategy labels the operator HLRs P1 while the RFE's multi-tenant artifact serving value materializes only when both land. The strategy's labels are respected as written; planning should note the coupling.\n- **Pending strategy-level process gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and the other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\" at strategy level; they gate the strategy, not individual epics.\n- **Latency target undefined (open question, PM/Engineering)**: no acceptable per-request validation overhead is set, and performance optimization is out of scope (separate RFE). E004 measures and reports the overhead; if a target is later set that requires caching at launch, plan a follow-up epic (short-TTL caching is the strategy's noted mitigation).\n- **Audit channel open question (PM/Security)**: HLR-8 specifies server logs. If PM/Security selects OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log instead, the audit-logging portion of E004 expands — re-plan that portion when the decision lands.\n- **Scope trap noted (S3-only path resolution)**: per the strategy's risk mitigation, initial path-to-workspace resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store layout (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation, not supported.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist (workspace store, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar); there is no uncertain-timing external dependency, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The three investigations run in parallel at the start; the server-side stream (E001 → E005 → E004) and the operator stream (E003 → E006) proceed independently.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64234,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1d420f326264026","agent_id":"a42a760e8aa834b42","session_id":"17a14eab-664e-4874-b0eb-335a5501da93","uuid":"ee8fc444-fb3c-4cad-aaed-9e35d0caf161","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:27:24.723Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
