{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d979592e-6e14-48a4-9a51-d55dae6a8e29","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a62ad4d8b515cff0","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-ks66mr2k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Investigate artifact request path resolvability to workspace membership\n\n## Description\n\nResolves whether artifact request paths handled by the artifacts-only serving endpoint carry sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership without initializing tracking stores. Artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths — for the standard S3 artifact store, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` — and a workspace-aware artifacts-only server must resolve each artifact path to its parent experiment and then to the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store. The outcome determines the workspace-validation approach in the MLflow server implementation. The strategy flags this both as an assumption needing validation (\"artifact request paths include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\") and as the risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\".\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace how artifact request paths are constructed and interpreted by the artifacts-only proxy for the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration), and confirm whether the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure reliably yields the parent experiment ID.\n- Determine whether run-ID-only paths can be resolved to their parent experiment without a tracking store, and what would be required if they cannot.\n- Identify artifact root configurations where path-to-workspace resolution is not reliable (custom artifact roots, non-S3 backends) and document them as limitations, per the strategy's mitigation of scoping the initial implementation to the S3 artifact store.\n- Deliver a validated path-resolution approach with pass/fail criteria that the server-side workspace validation implementation can build on.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written, code-referenced determination of whether artifact request paths include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership — a definitive result answering the strategy's assumption.\n- A validated approach for resolving an artifact request path to workspace membership in a server context without tracking stores, demonstrated against the S3 artifact store layout.\n- A documented list of artifact-store configurations where resolution is not reliable, with each limitation stated so it can be carried into the implementation's documentation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-1 and HLR-2 (P0, workspace-validated artifact serving) by determining the path-resolution approach the server implementation builds on.\n- Derives from the strategy's assumption \"Artifact request paths in the `/api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/*` endpoints include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership\" and the risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\".\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":80362,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1d420f326264026","agent_id":"a42a760e8aa834b42","session_id":"17a14eab-664e-4874-b0eb-335a5501da93","uuid":"6f0a58cb-b40c-4e20-8e36-052ded3277c6","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:27:40.218Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
