{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82679f0517063aa4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If artifact request paths cannot be resolved to workspace membership,\n16\t    re-scope workspace validation to the supported artifact-store layouts, document\n17\t    the non-resolvable configurations as limitations, and re-plan the resolution approach\n18\t    before implementing.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tMakes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths such as `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests that fail workspace validation receive HTTP 403. The design is fail-closed: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation, so transient infrastructure failures cannot silently degrade tenant isolation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags, per Staff Engineer / SME input. This epic builds on the plugin workspace resolution capability for the artifacts-only context and on the validated artifact path resolution approach, both delivered elsewhere in this strategy.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (the server fails to start with a clear error when it is missing) and initialize only the workspace provider.\n45\t- Keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized in this mode; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n46\t- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment, look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny with HTTP 403 on failed workspace validation.\n47\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n48\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unaffected — no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change in meaning.\n49\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (HLR-8, P2 — bundled here because it instruments the same artifact-request denial path the P0 validation work builds; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface).\n50\t- Measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation to inform the pending product decision on acceptable overhead (the strategy sets no latency target; performance optimization is out of scope).\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Starting the server with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` and no `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error.\n55\t- With `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, the server initializes only the workspace provider; tracking and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n56\t- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n57\t- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n58\t- With the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n59\t- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved), verified by the single-tenant / backwards-compatibility regression suite.\n60\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit log entries in the server log.\n61\t- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported in this epic's test report (no target is set by the strategy; the measurement informs the pending product decision).\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving.\n66\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members are denied with HTTP 403.\n67\t- HLR-3 (P0): workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable.\n68\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes.\n69\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs — bundled, not separately schedulable: it instruments the same artifact-request denial path built by the P0 validation work; splitting would double-touch the same request-handling surface and endpoints.\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-857b1162f4264d94","agent_id":"a733d258d89a3b029","session_id":"17a14eab-664e-4874-b0eb-335a5501da93","uuid":"b085bcb2-912a-41a3-b76b-c13ff4386148","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:30:52.089Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-82679f0517063aa4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
