{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aee435315d5157fb","type":"tool_result","content":"5:- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/mlflow.git\n16:**Detailed**: MLflow is deployed as a containerized tracking server on port 5000, serving the MLflow UI, REST API, and artifact proxy behind the `kubernetes-auth` authentication plugin from the external `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package (v1.5.0). The server runs Python 3.12 under gunicorn/uvicorn on a UBI9-minimal base image, with Kubernetes service account bearer tokens providing authentication and the `mlflow/server/auth` module enforcing RBAC-based authorization with roles, permissions, and per-resource access control. The `--enable-workspaces` flag activates multi-tenant workspace isolation backed by a Kubernetes workspace store (`kubernetes://`), where each workspace maps to a Kubernetes namespace. The component surface includes 6 architecture components—the container entrypoint, gunicorn and uvicorn ASGI/WSGI servers, the mlflow and mlflow-skinny Python services, and the mlflow-tracing SDK—exposing 48 HTTP endpoints for experiment tracking, model registry, gateway proxy, assistant, job execution, and MCP server APIs. External integrations include S3-compatible storage via boto3, Google Cloud Storage, and the Kubernetes API for project execution as Jobs. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:49-58, 76-80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:203-258, mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49]\n20:MLflow operates in RHOAI as a multi-tenant experiment tracking and model registry server. The container entrypoint (`Dockerfile.konflux:80`) starts the MLflow tracking server with three key flags that define its RHOAI-specific behavior: `--app-name kubernetes-auth` loads the server-side authentication plugin from the separately packaged `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` (v1.5.0, installed from PyPI via hermetic Konflux build), `--enable-workspaces` activates namespace-scoped workspace isolation, and `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` delegates workspace storage to the Kubernetes API. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13]\n22:The authentication architecture has two layers. On the server side, the `--app-name kubernetes-auth` entry point loads an auth middleware plugin that intercepts API requests. On the client side, `mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py` provides two auth providers activated via `MLFLOW_TRACKING_AUTH`: `kubernetes` (bearer token only) and `kubernetes-namespaced` (bearer token plus `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header derived from the pod's service account namespace). Both resolve credentials from the Kubernetes service account token file at `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token`, falling back to kubeconfig when running outside a cluster. The core `mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py` module provides RBAC enforcement with role-based permissions, per-resource access control for experiments, registered models, gateway endpoints, and workspaces. [source: mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:1-11, 38-41, 203-294, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226]\n30:### Repo Lineage\n32:| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n261:| Tracking Server API | All | kubernetes-auth plugin (Kubernetes service account bearer token) | Flask application via mlflow-kubernetes-plugins entry point | RBAC with role-based per-resource permissions |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-857b1162f4264d94","agent_id":"a733d258d89a3b029","session_id":"17a14eab-664e-4874-b0eb-335a5501da93","uuid":"7196ef46-e947-4d86-b7b0-904ad96ef247","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:32:02.029Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aee435315d5157fb","permiss