{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-954d206ef3a93d73","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8009\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8009` exited 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and epic-size re-split triggers all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition), so the 6-epic output is expected; no contradiction. No conditional branch files exist, and none are required. Graded below is only what the validator cannot check.\n17\t\n18\t## Criterion Details\n19\t\n20\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n21\tAll 8 HLRs from the strategy map to at least one epic, verified against each epic's HLR Traceability section: HLR-1 through HLR-4 (P0) → E004; HLR-5, HLR-6 (P1) → E006; HLR-7 (P1) → E005, correctly raised to P0 by priority inheritance since it enables the P0 workspace-membership validation (HLR-2); HLR-8 (P2, audit logging) → E004, explicitly bundled with a sound justification — it instruments the same artifact-request denial path the P0 validation work builds, making it incidental polish on the P0 work rather than a distinct deferrable feature. Priority inheritance is clean: E005 (P0) gates only P0 work (E004), and the P1 epics E003/E006 block no P0 work. The traceability matrix is present, complete, and matches the epic bodies. The audit-channel open question (possible OTLP/K8s-audit-log expansion of HLR-8) is carried as a re-plan trigger in Health Warnings rather than silently dropped.\n22\t\n23\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n24\tCross-checked every edge in both directions: the diagram's four edges (E001→E005, E002→E004, E005→E004, E003→E006) exactly match the frontmatter `dependencies` lists, arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention, no cycles, and no edge references a nonexistent epic. All three investigation gates are true gates with `rewrite` failure actions. The E005→E004 edge is a justified library→consumer dependency — the server's per-request validation calls the plugin's workspace resolution API, a code-level build-against relationship, not a configuration reference. E004∥E006 is correctly kept parallel: the operator consumes the server's flag combination as configuration rendered into Deployment specs, expressed as AC gates on E006 (rendered container command, end-to-end routing check) rather than a DAG edge — the rule-compliant shape. Critical path length 3 (E001→E005→E004) is consistent with the summary's `critical_path_length: 3` and reasonable for an M-L strategy structured as three parallel investigations feeding two independent implementation streams. No data/artifact dependency discoverable in epic content is missing from the DAG; the one potential cross-stream contingency (E003 concluding the tracking server needs code changes) is explicitly recorded as a re-plan trigger in both E006's `gate_failure_impact` and the Health Warnings, which is the correct handling for a conditionally-scoped outcome.\n25\t\n26\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n27\tAll work belongs to a single team (MLflow Team), so no team-boundary violations. The Component Mapping is honest and verified: `.context/rhai-components.txt` contains `MLflow` but neither `MLflow Operator` nor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`, so filing all six epics under the canonical `MLflow` component with team-based routing is the best available mapping and is documented as such. No scope duplication: E004 (server request-handling/initialization) and E005 (plugin initialization path) touch distinct surfaces with distinct deliverables, and E006 is disjoint operator work. No work-product type mixing — every epic is code or investigation; the P2 audit logging is code (log statements on the denial path), not content authoring. The E006 size overage (2-3 sprints in the strategy's estimate, above the ~2-week guideline) is handled exactly per the carve-out: acknowledged as one logical change to the operator's Helm chart with a concrete, measurable re-split trigger recorded in Health Warnings (re-split along the HLR-5/HLR-6 boundary if chart work proves materially larger than estimated or if the routing investigation concludes the tracking server needs code changes). A triggered, justified overage — criterion not docked.\n28\t\n29\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n30\tAll three Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure: E001 determines verify-vs-refactor for E005 (and transitively E004), E002 determines the workspace-validation approach in E004, E003 determines the HTTPRoute design and routing configuration in E006. Each has downstream dependents. All three Implementations produce concrete artifacts (server code, plugin code, operator chart templates and validation). Gating metadata is fully correct: every `gated_by` target (E004→E002, E005→E001, E006→E003) appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list; every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach`; no epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by` (E004's other dependency, E005, is an Implementation, so no gate is required).\n31\t\n32\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n33\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: the three Investigations carry `investigation_signals` (null `ai_signals`), the three Implementations carry `ai_signals` (null `investigation_signals`) — no cross-contamination. Signal values cross-checked against the strategy content, the rationale files, and `.context`: E001's `local_runnability: 0` (kubernetes:// store needs a cluster or API-server stand-in) vs E002's `1` (path handling exercisable as a local pip library) is a correct distinction; E003's `question_specificity: 0` honestly reflects a design-selection criterion rather than pass/fail; E005's `repo_access: 0` is well-calibrated — mlflow.md confirms the plugin is consumed as a published PyPI package (v1.5.0) with no source repository listed; E004's `repo_access: 1` and E006's `repo_access: 1` are grounded in the respective repo lineages (red-hat-data-services/mlflow, red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator). The `open_questions: -1` values on all three Implementations are consistent with their investigation gates. `architecture_claims: 1` on all three Implementation epics cites corroborating architecture-context evidence (Dockerfile.konflux CMD, entry-point registration, Helm-in-operator pattern) verified present in mlflow.md and mlflow-operator.md — no calibration misses. Computed scores were not re-derived, per the review rules.\n34\t\n35\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n36\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: E004's eight ACs trace directly to strategy acceptance criteria 1-4 (HTTP 200 member, HTTP 403 non-member, HTTP 503 fail-closed, no-workspace-flag backwards compatibility via regression suite) plus the fail-to-start error and audit-log ACs; E006's six ACs cover strategy criteria 5-6 (distinct Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute resources, exact rendered container command) plus routing, misconfiguration rejection, conditional CRD-gated HTTPRoute creation, and reconciliation idempotency. The flag-gated activation design makes backwards compatibility the rollback story, and it is explicitly covered. No rule-mandated AC is missing (no konflux-chain, docs-authoring, or replacement epics in this set). No AC states an externally-controlled precondition — the latency AC requires measurement and reporting of work the epic performs, not a schedule or access granted by others. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic couplings are described functionally (\"delivered elsewhere in this strategy\", \"per the routing design selected under this strategy\").\n37\t\n38\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n39\tFull scope coverage: server-side flag composition and validation (E004), plugin artifacts-only resolution (E005), operator split-server topology with routing (E006), and all three strategy unknowns that change epic structure became Investigations (E001-E003). The remaining open questions are correctly dispositioned with documented reasoning: latency (out of scope per strategy, E004 carries a measurement AC and a Health Warning re-plan trigger) and audit channel (HLR-8 as written specifies server logs, with a re-plan trigger if PM/Security selects otherwise). Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all four risks map to epic scope or Health Warnings (S3-only path-resolution scope trap, plugin coupling investigation, operator validation against misconfiguration, latency measurement), all five \"needs validation\" assumptions are dispositioned in the Investigation Derivation table, and the dependency notes (workspace-store fail-to-start, conditional Gateway API, unchanged kube-rbac-proxy) appear as ACs or scope items. All three Staff Engineer overrides are respected: no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` field anywhere, E006 is a genuine split deployment, and no epic dictates SSAR/Kubernetes authorization mechanics. Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not \"Yes\"), so no docs-authoring epic is required — confirmed by the contract validator's Rule 11 check. No conditional branches are needed; all outcomes are handled via gates and re-plan triggers within a single plan.\n40\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3e53e95f47b801","agent_id":"a37e89ccbd98eabaa","session_id":"17a14eab-664e-4874-b0eb-335a5501da93","uuid":"b6cde520-27f5-4da6-8e8f-6721423d02dd","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:33:54.737Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-954d206ef3a93d73","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept